`importers: Set<object>` and `importedModules: Set<object>` cause a TypeScript type error at the recursive call sites. When iterating these sets (lines 85–95), each element has type `object`, which is not assignable to the structured parameter type `{ file: string | null; type: ...; importers: Set<object>; importedModules: Set<object>; }`. TypeScript's structural typing cannot narrow `object` to a specific shape, so `getOrCreateModuleNode(importer)` and `getOrCreateModuleNode(importedModule)` will produce compile errors.

Note that the test file already defines the correct fix — a self-referential `ViteModuleNodeLike` type:
```typescript
type ViteModuleNodeLike = {
  file: string | null;
  type: ModuleNode['type'];
  importers: Set<ViteModuleNodeLike>;
  importedModules: Set<ViteModuleNodeLike>;
};
```
Define this same type locally inside `buildModuleGraph` (or at module scope) and use it as the parameter type for `getOrCreateModuleNode`, and as the generic for the `WeakMap` on line 45.

The `setInterval` callback is `async`, but `setInterval` does not handle the returned Promise. If `server.waitForRequestsIdle()` on line 162 throws, the error becomes an unhandled promise rejection that crashes the Node process. The 1-second polling loop is effectively a fire-and-forget. Wrap the body in a `try/catch`:
```typescript
waitForModuleGraph = setInterval(() => {
  void (async () => {
    try {
      if (server.moduleGraph.fileToModulesMap.size > 0) {
        clearInterval(waitForModuleGraph);
        waitForModuleGraph = undefined;
        await server.waitForRequestsIdle();
        watcherChangeHandler?.();
      }
    } catch (e) {
      logger.warn('[builder-vite] waitForRequestsIdle failed:', e);
    }
  })();
}, 1000);
```
